  "conclusion": "Rolling-horizon MPC spends less energy than the one-step policies at every thickness (1448 / 1602 / 2890 against dual 1620 / 1864 / 3308) because it pre-empts the heat before the target is crossed. It also stops earlier on thick pieces, which costs progress: at 30 mm it stops at 1378 s against dual 1866 s and returns e=-0.833 instead of +0.146. The cause is shared with the scalar tracker: the controller optimises on the same biased cold-spot point estimate, so a better decision rule does not fix a biased input. That is the honest reading of this comparison and it is why the paper's main claim is about information, not about the optimiser."
